WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.996
Welcome to Cauldron Lane!

2
00:00:03.996 --> 00:00:09.964
In this video you will learn how to play by watching a real game against the computer, step by step.

3
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:17.129
You run a potion shop on a busy lane: buy herbs, hunt magical creatures for rare parts,

4
00:00:17.129 --> 00:00:21.483
brew potions, and sell them to the townsfolk, or to the shop next door.

5
00:00:21.483 --> 00:00:26.647
Every action costs days, and when the calendar reaches the Moon Fair on day twenty-eight,

6
00:00:26.647 --> 00:00:28.455
the richest shop wins.

7
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.793
This is the home page, with buttons for the game lobby and the music.

8
00:00:35.793 --> 00:00:41.000
First pick your opponents: computer shops, or up to four players sharing this device.

9
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.957
Online play comes at launch.

10
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.259
You can face one to three rival shops. One is the easiest to follow.

11
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.700
Name your shop and pick the difficulty.

12
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:58.369
Hard plans every day carefully, and opens with three extra coins. We will take it on.

13
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:04.909
We will open first. Below are the rulebook and your game history. Let’s open the shops!

14
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.409
At the top: day zero of twenty-eight, the next market day, and the Moon Fair.

15
00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:12.160
Below are the two shops.

16
00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:18.576
We start with fifteen coins; Blue Phial gets one more for going second, and three for playing on Hard.

17
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:21.980
This is the calendar.

18
00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:28.189
Every action moves your pawn forward by its days, and whoever is furthest behind always moves next.

19
00:01:28.189 --> 00:01:33.183
Every even day has a doorbell that brings a customer; carts mark market days;

20
00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:35.567
day twenty-eight is the Moon Fair.

21
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.260
The market sells four common ingredients on price ladders.

22
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:46.972
You always take the cheapest token left, so prices climb until market day restocks them.

23
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:52.761
The bestiary shows creature sightings: a hunt costs days, not coins.

24
00:01:52.761 --> 00:01:57.989
The library’s recipes each exist once: buy one and only your shop can brew it.

25
00:01:57.989 --> 00:02:00.501
And the Guild buys any potion, cheaply.

26
00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.695
On the right is your shop: your actions, then your waiting customers.

27
00:02:05.695 --> 00:02:10.624
Each wants a kind of potion and shows what they pay: eleven coins for a common,

28
00:02:10.624 --> 00:02:16.464
nineteen for a Rare, plus a tip. Ours, Lady Vane and Old Mortimer, both want healing.

29
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.241
Below are your shelf, pantry and recipe book.

30
00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:25.595
Every shop knows the four common recipes, plus one Rare of its own.

31
00:02:25.595 --> 00:02:29.152
Ours is the Unicorn Remedy: only we can brew it.

32
00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.000
On your move you take one action, and each button shows its cost in days.

33
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.772
A Healing Draught takes two Silverleaf and a Starlight dew, and our pantry is empty.

34
00:02:40.772 --> 00:02:42.473
So, off to market!

35
00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.095
You can buy up to six tokens per trip.

36
00:02:46.095 --> 00:02:50.128
The one-coin spaces start empty, so these cost two coins each.

37
00:02:50.128 --> 00:02:56.117
Two Silverleaf and a dew for the Healing Draught, then two Moonstone and a Glowcap for a Warding Tonic.

38
00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.564
Why a Warding Tonic?

39
00:02:59.564 --> 00:03:04.024
Both of Blue Phial’s customers want protection, so Blue might buy ours.

40
00:03:04.024 --> 00:03:07.091
The trip takes one day. Twelve coins: buy!

41
00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.620
Now Blue Phial is furthest behind, on day zero, so it moves.

42
00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.879
Its moves pop up at the bottom, and go in the ledger.

43
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:22.271
Blue Phial went to market, for 16 coins. Then, still behind us on day one,

44
00:03:22.271 --> 00:03:24.960
it brewed a Healing Draught and a Warding Tonic,

45
00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.091
and Grum bought the Warding Tonic for 13 coins.

46
00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:33.363
Our move. Brewing makes one or two potions, in two days.

47
00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.831
We pick the Healing Draught.

48
00:03:35.831 --> 00:03:42.140
A waiting customer will buy it as soon as it’s on the shelf, and pays by quality, whatever the tag says.

49
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:47.823
Then the Warding Tonic. Its price tag is what another shop pays for it.

50
00:03:47.823 --> 00:03:53.727
Blue’s customer would pay Blue thirteen, so the game suggests ten: a profit for both of us.

51
00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:56.616
Let’s brew!

52
00:03:58.880 --> 00:03:59.905
Sold!

53
00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:06.179
Old Mortimer leaves soonest, so he buys the Healing Draught: eleven coins, plus a two-coin tip.

54
00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:08.691
Lady Vane will wait until day eight.

55
00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:16.405
Our pawn also passed the day-two doorbell: Sir Roland the knight wants a Rare healing potion, or better.

56
00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.537
Our Unicorn Remedy is a Rare healing potion.

57
00:04:20.537 --> 00:04:25.311
Tap any recipe, in your book or in the library, to see everything it needs.

58
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.281
It takes a Unicorn hair, a Silverleaf and a Starlight dew.

59
00:04:29.281 --> 00:04:32.696
For each one you see where it comes from and whether you have it.

60
00:04:32.696 --> 00:04:37.021
Only our shop can brew it, and Sir Roland would pay us twenty-one coins.

61
00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.300
Both pawns are on day three.

62
00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.077
When they share a day, the one that arrived last moves first — us.

63
00:04:43.077 --> 00:04:47.232
Changing a price tag is free; our Warding Tonic is tagged at ten.

64
00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:53.700
Lady Vane still wants healing, but Blue Phial has a Healing Draught on its shelf.

65
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:58.196
Shopping at a rival buys up to two potions at their tags, in one day.

66
00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.580
Blue tagged it at eight coins.

67
00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:05.436
Lady Vane will pay us eleven, so that’s a three-coin profit.

68
00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.055
Bought! It lands on our shelf, and Lady Vane snaps it up for eleven coins.

69
00:05:11.055 --> 00:05:08.240
Now it is Blue Phial’s move.

70
00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.316
On Blue Phial’s move, the calendar reached day four: market day.

71
00:05:12.316 --> 00:05:18.568
The market restocks, Guild prices recover, and the oldest creature wanders off while a new one is sighted.

72
00:05:18.568 --> 00:05:22.339
A pair of unicorns! The library swaps a recipe, too.

73
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:31.948
Then Blue Phial went to market. Now look at our banner: just before market day,

74
00:05:31.948 --> 00:05:36.301
Blue Phial bought our Warding Tonic for ten coins, for its own customer.

75
00:05:36.301 --> 00:05:38.216
The coins came straight to us.

76
00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:44.440
Now for those unicorns. Tap a sighting in the bestiary to plan a hunt.

77
00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.489
A pair of unicorns takes four days: three, plus one for the pair.

78
00:05:49.489 --> 00:05:53.260
It brings back two Unicorn hairs, enough for two remedies.

79
00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.495
On the way, our pawn passes two doorbells, but a queue only holds three customers.

80
00:05:59.495 --> 00:06:01.068
Let’s go hunting.

81
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:08.281
Pip the bard came in on day six, but on day eight the queue was full, so that customer walked on by.

82
00:06:08.281 --> 00:06:03.720
Now Blue Phial gets several turns in a row.

83
00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:07.007
Blue Phial brewed a Healing Draught and a Sleeping Tonic,

84
00:06:07.007 --> 00:06:10.464
and Shady Nell bought the Sleeping Tonic for eleven coins.

85
00:06:10.464 --> 00:06:14.689
Then it bought the recipe for the Invisibility Draught, for seven coins.

86
00:06:14.689 --> 00:06:16.625
Now only Blue can brew it.

87
00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.987
Then the calendar reached day eight: another market day.

88
00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.720
Blue Phial went to market, for seven coins.

89
00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:32.236
Our move. We have the Unicorn hairs, but need two Silverleaf and two Starlight dew.

90
00:06:32.236 --> 00:06:35.708
After the restock, all four cost just five coins.

91
00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:43.201
Blue Phial was already on day nine, so we arrived last and move again.

92
00:06:43.201 --> 00:06:45.479
Let’s brew two Unicorn Remedies.

93
00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.529
Sir Roland pays twenty-one coins for his Rare potion.

94
00:06:52.529 --> 00:06:58.483
And Old Mortimer is back, wanting any healing potion, so he takes the other remedy for nineteen.

95
00:06:58.483 --> 00:07:00.525
Forty coins from one brew!

96
00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.215
Better potions pay far more, and since only we know this recipe,

97
00:07:06.215 --> 00:07:09.900
any shop with a Rare healing customer has to buy from us.

98
00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.255
Meanwhile, with our pawn on day eleven, Blue Phial caught up.

99
00:07:15.255 --> 00:07:21.869
It brewed Night-Eye Drops and a Warding Tonic, and Shady Nell bought the Night-Eye Drops for 13 coins.

100
00:07:21.869 --> 00:07:27.517
Then it spent three days hunting a basilisk, and Grum bought the Warding Tonic for eleven coins.

101
00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:34.663
A few more actions. Buy a recipe costs coins and a day. Phoenix Tears,

102
00:07:34.663 --> 00:07:37.096
a Mythic healing potion, costs seven,

103
00:07:37.096 --> 00:07:40.888
and the dialog shows what it pays and which creatures it needs.

104
00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:44.903
Mind the shop earns one coin a day,

105
00:07:44.903 --> 00:07:48.232
and moves your pawn to one day past the next pawn ahead:

106
00:07:48.232 --> 00:07:50.765
handy when nothing else is worth doing.

107
00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:56.056
And Sell to the Guild turns your shelf into coins, in one day.

108
00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.287
Everything is explained in the rulebook, one click away from the Rules button,

109
00:08:01.287 --> 00:08:03.564
in chapters like a real rulebook.

110
00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:08.932
There are chapters on the calendar, on every action and its cost in days,

111
00:08:08.932 --> 00:08:11.849
and on customers and selling to other shops.

112
00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.783
Ingredients, creatures and recipes lists every beast and every potion,

113
00:08:17.783 --> 00:08:21.255
and the last chapters cover the Moon Fair and a quick reference.

114
00:08:23.204 --> 00:08:27.003
Let’s skip to the end. When every pawn reaches day twenty-eight,

115
00:08:27.003 --> 00:08:31.669
the Guild buys every potion left on a shelf, and the richest shop wins.

116
00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:38.952
The totals stay hidden until the Guild has counted the coins, so nothing gives the result away early.

117
00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:44.532
So close! Blue Phial won by three coins, 92 to 89.

118
00:08:44.532 --> 00:08:50.009
It started with four more, so we actually made more profit, but on Hard every coin counts.

119
00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:55.424
The ledger shows where each shop’s money came from, and what it spent.

120
00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:02.883
From here you can look back at the lane, play again, or see your game history.

121
00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:09.281
Remember: watch your customers’ patience, price your shelf for the shop next door,

122
00:09:09.281 --> 00:09:11.779
and count the days each action costs.

123
00:09:11.779 --> 00:09:16.303
Your game saves as you play, and you can keep several going from the game lobby.

124
00:09:16.303 --> 00:09:18.324
Good luck, and happy brewing!

